Third-party material
--------------------

The dual license above covers this repository's own source code. Some files
are covered by their own terms instead, and those terms travel with them:

  * assets/cyw43-firmware/
      Radio firmware, NVRAM and CLM blobs for the Infineon/Broadcom CYW43439,
      used by the Raspberry Pi Pico W and Pico 2 W. Redistributed under the
      Permissive Binary License, Version 1.0 — see
      assets/cyw43-firmware/LICENSE-permissive-binary-license-1.0.txt.
      That license permits redistribution in binary form WITHOUT
      modification, provided the copyright notice and disclaimer are
      reproduced, and it prohibits reverse engineering. The IDE binary
      embeds these blobs to write them into Pico W projects, so a binary
      distribution of the IDE must reproduce that notice as well.

  * assets/fpga-gateware/pico2-ice/pico2_ice.pcf
      tinyVision.ai's pin catalogue for the pico2-ice, from pico-ice-sdk,
      under the MIT license in LICENSE-tinyvision-MIT.txt beside it. The IDE
      binary embeds it and copies it into every pico2-ice project.

  * src/panels/mcu_module/testdata/rgb_blink.bin
      tinyVision.ai's factory blink bitstream, used only by tests, under the
      MIT license in LICENSE-tinyvision-MIT.txt beside it.

Building this application also links Rust crates and, through libusb1-sys,
the C library libusb, under their own licenses, chiefly MIT and Apache-2.0.
Three are worth naming:

  * serialport is MPL-2.0. That is file-level copyleft: modifications to its
    own source files must be shared under MPL-2.0, but distributing it as
    part of a larger work under different terms is expressly permitted. A
    binary distribution must say where its source can be obtained.

  * libusb1-sys (pulled in by rusb) declares MIT, but when no libusb is found
    through vcpkg or pkg-config it compiles its bundled libusb 1.0.27
    (LGPL-2.1-or-later) and links it statically, which is the usual case on
    Windows. A binary distribution must include the LGPL-2.1 text and a
    notice that it contains libusb, provide libusb's source with it (or from
    the same place), and allow relinking against a modified libusb.

  * epaint_default_fonts bundles fonts under MIT + Bitstream Vera (Hack),
    OFL-1.1 (Noto Emoji) and the Ubuntu Font License 1.0; egui-phosphor
    embeds the Phosphor icons (MIT, under their own copyright); on Linux,
    sctk-adwaita also embeds Cantarell under OFL-1.1. Their notices must be
    preserved in binary distributions.
